{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Navigation charts often rely on inductive stability of geography, whereas Popperian science explicitly denies the stability of natural laws.","A navigator seeks the highest probability of safety, while the Popperian scientist seeks the highest degree of corroboration without assigning probability."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that avoiding known errors provides no advantage in survival/success over random choice in a chaotic environment.","If the concept of 'known error' is shown to be temporally unstable (i.e., a theory falsified yesterday is valid today)."],"shared_structure":["Both scenarios involve making high-stakes decisions with incomplete information about the future.","Both rely on eliminating known dangers (charted rocks vs. falsified theories) rather than guaranteeing a safe path.","Both accept that the chosen path may still fail due to unknown factors, but rejecting the known-safe path would be irrational."],"source_memory_refs":["naval_navigation_chart"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a captain prefers a chart with fewer known errors even without knowing the future weather, a scientist prefers a theory with fewer known falsifications even without knowing future test results.","The rationality of the choice lies in the quality of the map/theory relative to known errors, not in the certainty of the destination/outcome."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for the better-corroborated theory is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive 'critical preference' grounded in verisimilitude and risk-avoidance, but accepting this defense costs the agent any rational assurance of future success, reducing scientific action to a bold gamble that avoids known falsehoods without promising truth.","counterconditions":["If the preference for the better-corroborated theory implicitly assigns a higher subjective probability of future success, it collapses into the induction Popper rejected.","If Popper's formal definition of verisimilitude is logically incoherent (as argued by Tichý and Miller), then it cannot serve as a rational basis for preference.","If acting on a theory inherently requires an expectation of regularity (that the future will resemble the past), then any 'risk-management' strategy is merely disguised pragmatism."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument reconstructs Popper's distinction between 'corroboration' (a logical report of past performance) and 'probability' (an inductive prediction). It posits that choosing the best-corroborated theory is not an inference that the theory will hold, but a methodological rule to minimize the risk of acting on a theory already known to be false. The mechanism relies on the concept of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) to argue that while we cannot know a theory is true, a highly corroborated theory has withstood attempts to show it is less like the truth than its rivals. Thus, the preference is rational not because it predicts success, but because it systematically eliminates known errors, leaving the 'least bad' option for action.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}